<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cesare Beccaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cesare Beccaria (1738–1794) was an Italian jurist, philosopher and economist of the Milanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His treatise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments">On Crimes and Punishments</a> (1764) condemned torture and the death penalty and argued that punishments should be proportionate and aimed at preventing crime rather than at revenge. It influenced penal reform across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including Tuscany's abolition of the death penalty in 1786.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesare-beccaria.jpg" alt="Cesare Beccaria" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eliseo Sala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cesare_Beccaria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cesare Beccaria was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 15 March 1738 to an aristocrat of moderate standing. He was educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college in Parma and graduated in law from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pavia">University of Pavia</a> in 1758. Reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> turned his attention to economics, and in 1762 he published a tract on the disorder of the currency in the Milanese states. In his mid-twenties he became close friends with Pietro and Alessandro Verri, and with other young Milanese aristocrats they formed a literary society, the Academy of Fists. Much of its talk concerned reform of criminal justice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1764, encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-verri">Pietro Verri</a>, Beccaria published <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-crimes-and-punishments">On Crimes and Punishments</a>, a brief treatise that marked the high point of the Milan Enlightenment. Alessandro Verri, a Milan prison official, knew the prison's appalling conditions at first hand. The treatise protested against torture to obtain confessions, secret accusations, the arbitrary discretion of judges and capital punishment for serious and even minor offences. It was the first full work of penology. Beccaria published it anonymously, fearing a backlash, and acknowledged it only once the government's approval was clear.</p><p>He argued from the social contract and from utility. Punishment, he held, should prevent crime rather than avenge it, should be proportionate to the crime, and should be prompt. A high probability of punishment, not its severity, achieves prevention, and laws should be clear so that judges decide only whether a law has been broken. He condemned the death penalty because the state has no right to take lives and because it is neither useful nor necessary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The treatise was translated into French and English almost at once, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> publicly endorsed it. Many penal reforms in the principal European nations can be traced to it, but few contemporaries accepted its argument against the death penalty. The Grand Duchy of Tuscany, the first state to abolish capital punishment, followed Beccaria's argument about its lack of utility, not about the state's lack of right. His thinking also shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a>'s utilitarianism. In 1768 a chair of law and economy was founded for him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. He died there on 28 November 1794, and his grandson was the novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-manzoni">Alessandro Manzoni</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cesare Beccaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Austrian Lombardy became a centre of the Italian Enlightenment: Pietro Verri’s journal Il Caffè and <b>Cesare Beccaria</b>’s On Crimes and Punishments (1764) influenced penal reform across Europe, and the Teatro alla Scala opened in 1778.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His criminal code of 30 November 1786, the Leopoldina, inspired by <b>Cesare Beccaria</b>, abolished torture, the crime of lèse-majesté and the death penalty, and is often described as the first modern legal code to abolish capital punishment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Cesare Beccaria</b>’s On Crimes and Punishments of 1764 condemned torture and the death penalty.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1774" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cesare Beccaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1774">What was here in 1774? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is better to prevent crimes than to punish them. This is the chief aim of every good system of legislation, which is the art of leading men to the greatest possible happiness or to the least possible misery…&#8221;</p><footer>On Crimes and Punishments (1764), chapter 41 &#183; tr. James Anson Farrer, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58700/pg58700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We shall see, if we open histories, that laws, which are or ought to be covenants between free men, have generally been nothing but the instrument of the passions of some few men, or the result of some accidental and temporary necessity.&#8221;</p><footer>On Crimes and Punishments (1764), introduction &#183; tr. James Anson Farrer, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58700/pg58700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The greater the number of those who understand and have in their hands the sacred code of the laws, the fewer will be the crimes committed…&#8221;</p><footer>On Crimes and Punishments (1764), chapter 5 &#183; tr. James Anson Farrer, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58700/pg58700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Capital punishment is injurious by the example of barbarity it presents.&#8221;</p><footer>On Crimes and Punishments (1764), chapter 28 &#183; tr. James Anson Farrer, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58700/pg58700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the history of mankind conveys to us the idea of an immense sea of errors, among which a few truths, confusedly and at long intervals, float on the surface.&#8221;</p><footer>On Crimes and Punishments (1764), chapter 28 &#183; tr. James Anson Farrer, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/58700/pg58700-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
